  summary: String.raw`A population changes only through births, deaths and moves, and every demographic rate divides a count of those events by the person-years lived by the people at risk of them. Growth compounds, so rates of one or two percent a year transform a population within a few generations. The Lexis diagram keeps age, period and cohort apart — although, because age = period − birth year, their separate effects can never be fully disentangled. Crude rates mix risks with age structure: standardization and Kitagawa's decomposition separate the two, explaining paradoxes such as a country with higher death rates at every age and a lower crude death rate. The chapter ends with the logistic forecasts of the 1920s and the lesson of their failure.`,
