      name: 'The demographic balancing equation',
      statement: String.raw`Let $P(t)$ be the number of people in a territory at time $t$. Over any interval $[t, t+n]$,
$$P(t+n) = P(t) + B - D + I - O,$$
where $B$ and $D$ are the births and deaths occurring in the territory during the interval and $I$ and $O$ are the people who move in (immigrants) and out (emigrants). $B - D$ is the **natural increase** and $I - O$ the **net migration**. The identity holds for any population with a definite membership rule: for a region the moves include those between regions, for an age group "entering" means reaching its lowest age and "leaving" includes reaching the next group. Dividing by the person-years lived during the interval (1.2) gives the same statement in rates: growth rate $=$ birth rate $-$ death rate $+$ net migration rate (1.3). When all five terms are measured independently, the amount by which the equation fails — the **error of closure** — measures the errors in the counts.`,
      idea: String.raw`It is bookkeeping. Everyone present at $t+n$ was either present at $t$ or arrived during the interval by birth or by immigration; everyone present at $t$ is either still present or left by death or by emigration. Count each person once on each side and the four flows are the only ways to change the total — the discrete form of the population balance law $dN/dt = \text{births} - \text{deaths} + \text{in} - \text{out}$ (mathbio 1.1). Because it is an identity it cannot be wrong, but it can be *used*: the component that is hardest to count, usually net migration, is often estimated as the residual $P(t+n) - P(t) - (B - D)$, and then it silently absorbs every error in the other counts.`,
      caption: String.raw`Pick a country and a year (or click the right panel). The tank holds the population on 1 January: births pour in at the left, deaths drain at the bottom, and net migration enters or leaves at the right, each stream's width proportional to its flow. Inside the tank the three flows are stacked as a waterfall from the old water line (dashed) to the new one — the top of the water is magnified, since a year's change is about 1% of the whole. Right: the flows every year since 1950, with the change in the population count (black) landing exactly on the net; "added up since 1950" shows which flow built today's population. Data: UN World Population Prospects 2024, which reconciles its estimates so that the equation closes — isolated jumps in its net-migration series (Germany 1998, 2006) are where corrections to the counts have been absorbed by the residual.`,
